# The history CSVs are appended to from both Windows (local runs) and Linux
# (GitHub Actions). Two rules keep a one-row append from becoming a
# whole-file diff:
#
#   1. The writers all pass lineterminator="\n" so new rows are LF.
#   2. Git is told NOT to convert line endings on these files (-text), so the
#      bytes already committed are left exactly as they are. Existing rows keep
#      whatever ending they were written with and are never rewritten.
#
# `diff` is set explicitly so Git still diffs them as text.
*.csv -text diff

# Source files are LF everywhere already; keep them that way.
*.py  text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.md  text eol=lf

# Shell scripts MUST be LF. They are edited on Windows and executed on the
# ubuntu-latest runner, and a CRLF shebang line makes Linux look for an
# interpreter literally named "bash\r" — the step dies with
# "/usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory", which reads like the
# runner is broken rather than like a line-ending problem.
#
# This is not covered by core.autocrlf alone: autocrlf is per-machine config,
# so it protects whoever has it set and nobody else. An attribute travels with
# the repo.
*.sh  text eol=lf
